WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.130
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4418.

2
00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:02.260
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.390
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Three, two, one . . .

4
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:12.805
<v TERESHA USSIN>Today is January twenty-third and I am Teresha Ussin and representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:12.805 --> 00:00:19.961
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

6
00:00:19.961 --> 00:00:29.000
And to kick that off, we are holding a Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.424
<v PATRICIA PRATER RUBIT>Rigmaiden.

8
00:00:29.424 --> 00:00:29.847
<v USSIN>Rigmaiden.

9
00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:30.695
Thank you.

10
00:00:30.695 --> 00:00:32.814
I’m here with Patricia Pratter . . .

11
00:00:32.814 --> 00:00:33.237
<v RUBIT>Prater.

12
00:00:33.237 --> 00:00:33.661
<v USSIN>Prater . . .

13
00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:34.085
<v RUBIT>Rubit.

14
00:00:34.085 --> 00:00:34.508
<v USSIN>Rubit.

15
00:00:34.508 --> 00:00:37.898
Thank you for agreeing to do the interview.

16
00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:42.983
This is going to be a short interview of about fifteen minutes,

17
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:54.000
and we will likely be in touch with you later to set up a much larger . . . Longer interview if you are interested in continuing on.

18
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:54.440
<v RUBIT>Okay.

19
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.280
<v USSIN>Well if you will again, could you state your full name?

20
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.920
<v RUBIT>My name is Patricia Prater Rubit.

21
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.360
<v USSIN>Okay.

22
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.000
And where were you born?

23
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.699
<v RUBIT>I was born in Lake Charles, Louisiana.

24
00:01:09.699 --> 00:01:11.712
Saint Patrick’s Hospital.

25
00:01:11.712 --> 00:01:17.753
<v USSIN>What were your parents’ names and your grandparents’ names?

26
00:01:17.753 --> 00:01:22.452
<v RUBIT>My father’s name was Elwood Prater, Senior.

27
00:01:22.452 --> 00:01:26.479
He was married to Ester Vincent.

28
00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:29.836
My grandfather was Austin Prater.

29
00:01:29.836 --> 00:01:33.863
He was married to Mittie Braxton.

30
00:01:33.863 --> 00:01:34.534
<v USSIN>Okay.

31
00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:43.260
<v RUBIT>My mother’s father was Roosevelt Vincent and he was married to Mamie Perkins.

32
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:49.973
And her . . . Mamie Perkins’ family was James and Sinnie Rigmaiden.

33
00:01:49.973 --> 00:01:52.658
<v USSIN>Those names, Braxton, Perkins . . .

34
00:01:52.658 --> 00:01:54.000
<v RUBIT>Rigmaiden.

35
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.500
<v USSIN>Rigmaiden, have come up a lot today.

36
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.000
<v RUBIT>Yes.

37
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.000
<v USSIN>Can you give us just a little . . . If you want, a little more background?

38
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.000
<v RUBIT>On the Rigmaiden side?

39
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.000
<v USSIN>[Agrees].

40
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.548
<v RUBIT>As I said, my great grandmother was a Rigmaiden.

41
00:02:13.548 --> 00:02:20.945
Her name was Sinnie Rigmaiden, and she was married to James Perkins.

42
00:02:20.945 --> 00:02:24.027
Josh Rigmaiden was Sinnie’s brother.

43
00:02:24.027 --> 00:02:27.110
They were brother and sister.

44
00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:32.658
So Josh Rigmaiden was actually my great great uncle.

45
00:02:32.658 --> 00:02:38.822
My dad’s mother was a Braxton . . . name was Mittie Braxton.

46
00:02:38.822 --> 00:02:41.904
Her father was Augustus Braxton.

47
00:02:41.904 --> 00:02:42.521
And . . .

48
00:02:42.521 --> 00:02:47.452
<v TANGLAO>Do you mind if I take your picture?

49
00:02:47.452 --> 00:02:48.068
<v RUBIT>Sure!

50
00:02:48.068 --> 00:02:53.000
<v USSIN>What is your earliest memory of Mossville?

51
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.763
<v RUBIT>Oh, my earliest memory of Mossville was . . . Oh, it dates back so long ago.

52
00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:07.972
We were . . . I can remember I must have been about four years old.

53
00:03:07.972 --> 00:03:16.844
I can remember my parents building the house that I actually spent . . . well, grew up in.

54
00:03:16.844 --> 00:03:25.161
As my family . . . My mother and father moved my family from Mossville in the mid-60s,

55
00:03:25.161 --> 00:03:31.261
1965 as a matter fact, and we went to San Francisco.

56
00:03:31.261 --> 00:03:42.905
Primarily because I’m one of ten children, and at that time the main reason was we were beginning to finish school.

57
00:03:42.905 --> 00:03:49.005
And so that was to further our education, going to college.

58
00:03:49.005 --> 00:03:56.213
After my father retired from San Francisco Municipal Railway, he moved back here.

59
00:03:56.213 --> 00:03:58.986
And subsequently, I moved back.

60
00:03:58.986 --> 00:04:01.758
But I grew up here.

61
00:04:01.758 --> 00:04:05.085
Went to school here at Mossville.

62
00:04:05.085 --> 00:04:11.185
As I said I can remember I must have been . . . Oh,

63
00:04:11.185 --> 00:04:18.948
it was preschool age when we moved into the house there on Burton Street.

64
00:04:18.948 --> 00:04:22.275
4016 and 4006 East Burton Street.

65
00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:26.711
My grandma . . . everyone . . . All my grandparents lived here.

66
00:04:26.711 --> 00:04:33.365
My uncles, aunts, extended family, so this holds a lot of history.

67
00:04:33.365 --> 00:04:42.237
I can remember growing up, it was always a . . . The community was a really wholesome community.

68
00:04:42.237 --> 00:04:47.227
Everyone worked together and everyone pretty much knew everyone.

69
00:04:47.227 --> 00:04:50.000
It was good times.

70
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.031
<v USSIN>Sounds like it.

71
00:04:51.031 --> 00:04:52.062
<v RUBIT>Very good times.

72
00:04:52.062 --> 00:04:53.094
<v USSIN>Sounds like it.

73
00:04:53.094 --> 00:04:53.438
<v RUBIT>Yes.

74
00:04:53.438 --> 00:04:54.125
It was.

75
00:04:54.125 --> 00:04:58.250
<v BILL SHEARMAN>Ms. Prater, would you mind giving us the year you were born?

76
00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.000
<v RUBIT>I was born in March seventeenth, 1947.

77
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:01.596
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

78
00:05:01.596 --> 00:05:02.788
Thank you.

79
00:05:02.788 --> 00:05:10.536
<v USSIN>And since you mentioned there was a closeness, a tight connection with family,

80
00:05:10.536 --> 00:05:23.053
where did the church and the community play a role . . . Who were some of the leaders in the church and why?

81
00:05:23.053 --> 00:05:27.821
<v RUBIT>I grew up in Mount Zion Baptist Church.

82
00:05:27.821 --> 00:05:39.146
As a matter of fact, my . . . one of my . . . A Braxton was the minister at the beginning of that.

83
00:05:39.146 --> 00:05:49.278
My grandfather was one of the first ushers that I knew at Mount Zion, Roosevelt Vincent, Senior.

84
00:05:49.278 --> 00:06:05.371
I have documentation, or the actual deed . . . A copy of the original deed in which that land was donated by the Moss family for the church there.

85
00:06:05.371 --> 00:06:12.523
I can remember the old church and when that was actually rebuilt.

86
00:06:12.523 --> 00:06:18.483
I think that was . . . Must have been in the 60s.

87
00:06:18.483 --> 00:06:23.848
Have some of the old pictures of that church.

88
00:06:23.848 --> 00:06:31.000
When I was a member there, Reverend White was the pastor.

89
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.786
<v USSIN>Where did you go to school?

90
00:06:33.786 --> 00:06:34.714
Elementary school?

91
00:06:34.714 --> 00:06:35.643
High school?

92
00:06:35.643 --> 00:06:36.107
<v RUBIT>Mossville.

93
00:06:36.107 --> 00:06:36.571
<v USSIN>Okay.

94
00:06:36.571 --> 00:06:42.607
Can you tell me where the young folks would gather in the community?

95
00:06:42.607 --> 00:06:50.964
<v RUBIT>The young folks would gather at . . . There was a place right across the street from the swimming pool,

96
00:06:50.964 --> 00:06:53.750
it was called Ms. Lula’s Place.

97
00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:57.929
That was . . . It was like a little teenage spot.

98
00:06:57.929 --> 00:07:02.107
We call it a canteen back at that time.

99
00:07:02.107 --> 00:07:07.214
And also down in the Bel Air area was Ms. Freelow’s.

100
00:07:07.214 --> 00:07:14.179
She had a . . . It was like a hamburger place and the youth would gather there.

101
00:07:14.179 --> 00:07:17.429
And also at the Recreation Center here.

102
00:07:17.429 --> 00:07:23.000
We would have games, and of course the pool and everything.

103
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:23.504
<v USSIN>Okay.

104
00:07:23.504 --> 00:07:31.070
And in the beginning of the interview you said that you just moved back, correct?

105
00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:31.574
<v RUBIT>Yes.

106
00:07:31.574 --> 00:07:32.078
<v USSIN>Okay.

107
00:07:32.078 --> 00:07:38.130
So knowing that you grew up here and now that you’re back,

108
00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:46.200
can you do like a comparison contrast of what you seen was different, was the same?

109
00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.209
<v RUBIT>Oh, dear.

110
00:07:47.209 --> 00:07:50.739
I was gone for about forty years.

111
00:07:50.739 --> 00:07:54.774
I lived in the San Francisco Bay area.

112
00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:59.817
And after I retired, I worked for . . . It’s now AT&amp;T.

113
00:07:59.817 --> 00:08:02.843
I was an engineer for AT&amp;T.

114
00:08:02.843 --> 00:08:06.374
Actually I was employed by Pacific Bell.

115
00:08:06.374 --> 00:08:11.922
At one time Pacific Bell was under the umbrella of AT&amp;T.

116
00:08:11.922 --> 00:08:19.991
And then they . . . I’m sure everybody knows about that breakup, and now they’re back together again.

117
00:08:19.991 --> 00:08:21.000
<v USSIN>[Agrees].

118
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:27.281
<v RUBIT>But once I worked for them for thirty years, and after I retired,

119
00:08:27.281 --> 00:08:32.596
my husband . . . After both of us retired, my dad was here.

120
00:08:32.596 --> 00:08:36.462
And I decided . . . We decided to move back.

121
00:08:36.462 --> 00:08:43.226
Now, I left in the mid-60s and there was quite a bit of change.

122
00:08:43.226 --> 00:08:49.024
But yes . . . when I left . . . Well, there’s a big span of time,

123
00:08:49.024 --> 00:09:02.070
but just remembering the way that I grew up and when I came back it was like everything was fragmented because people had migrated to other places.

124
00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:08.351
That real real close camaraderie that I grew up with wasn’t there anymore.

125
00:09:08.351 --> 00:09:13.666
I didn’t move back to . . . Actually I moved back to Mossville,

126
00:09:13.666 --> 00:09:25.262
and subsequently once my father was living I moved to Lake Charles, but once he deceased, back to the family property here in Mossville.

127
00:09:25.262 --> 00:09:28.161
So it was quite an adjustment.

128
00:09:28.161 --> 00:09:40.240
Just . . . it took me . . . Actually it took me about five years to adjust to being back here because it was that much of a change.

129
00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:46.038
As I said when I grew up my grandparents were all here.

130
00:09:46.038 --> 00:09:48.454
All my aunts and uncles.

131
00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:51.353
I grew up with my cousins.

132
00:09:51.353 --> 00:09:53.286
Very close knit family.

133
00:09:53.286 --> 00:10:00.050
As a matter of fact, we primarily lived in separate houses, but all together.

134
00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:05.365
And we would just . . . I had never seen anything like it.

135
00:10:05.365 --> 00:10:09.714
I mean we were just very very close . . . Visit.

136
00:10:09.714 --> 00:10:17.445
There were no . . . You didn’t have to worry about locking your doors or leaving anything out.

137
00:10:17.445 --> 00:10:21.793
I mean it was just a community based thing.

138
00:10:21.793 --> 00:10:26.142
You walked down the street everyone knew each other.

139
00:10:26.142 --> 00:10:33.389
Kids were very much disciplined and it was like the entire community raised the children.

140
00:10:33.389 --> 00:10:44.502
I mean it wasn’t just your parents and grandparents, but your aunts and uncles and the rest of the community that was involved.

141
00:10:44.502 --> 00:10:48.851
The school teachers, when I was in school, the . . .

142
00:10:48.851 --> 00:10:49.817
<v TANGLAO>The mic.

143
00:10:49.817 --> 00:10:51.267
<v RUBIT>Oh, I’m sorry.

144
00:10:51.267 --> 00:11:01.413
When I was in school the . . . As a matter of fact, the old school building I think was situated somewhere here.

145
00:11:01.413 --> 00:11:06.245
Right here in this spot, or almost in this spot.

146
00:11:06.245 --> 00:11:10.111
And it was a big building, two classrooms.

147
00:11:10.111 --> 00:11:21.707
Then of course they moved . . . They built the school that’s still existing down the way which was open to first through the twelfth grade.

148
00:11:21.707 --> 00:11:32.337
And as I said I graduated from high school in 1965, which is the year that my family actually relocated to California.

149
00:11:32.337 --> 00:11:42.000
<v USSIN>What is the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville that isn’t in the history books?

150
00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:42.649
<v RUBIT>Everything.

151
00:11:42.649 --> 00:11:46.543
The community, how everyone worked together.

152
00:11:46.543 --> 00:11:51.085
It was a cohesive . . . It wasn’t incorporated,

153
00:11:51.085 --> 00:12:06.660
but I can remember as a little girl that my Uncle Josh Rigmaiden was more like a mayor, but he wasn’t actually a mayor.

154
00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:09.904
He did have a store.

155
00:12:09.904 --> 00:12:18.989
And I can just remember a sense of togetherness, and everything was like harmonious.

156
00:12:18.989 --> 00:12:23.532
The neighborhood was well kept and manicured.

157
00:12:23.532 --> 00:12:31.319
The housing . . . it was like everyone was . . . had . . . How would I say?

158
00:12:31.319 --> 00:12:35.862
Some pride or proud . . . Just the families.

159
00:12:35.862 --> 00:12:39.755
<v USSIN>Did your family have a garden?

160
00:12:39.755 --> 00:12:43.000
Did you raise livestock?

161
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:43.486
<v RUBIT>Yes.

162
00:12:43.486 --> 00:12:45.914
My father had a garden.

163
00:12:45.914 --> 00:12:46.400
<v USSIN>Okay.

164
00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:47.857
<v RUBIT>A vegetable garden.

165
00:12:47.857 --> 00:12:48.343
<v USSIN>Okay.

166
00:12:48.343 --> 00:13:00.000
<v RUBIT>And an abundance of fruit trees, and nut trees, and . . . My father did a lot of hunting, and fishing, and gardening of course.

167
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.241
<v USSIN>That’s what I hear from a lot of folks that have been interviewed today is you could live off the land.

168
00:13:07.241 --> 00:13:07.586
<v RUBIT>Right.

169
00:13:07.586 --> 00:13:10.000
<v USSIN>It was a very rich land.

170
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:11.462
<v RUBIT>Yes it was.

171
00:13:11.462 --> 00:13:12.438
Very rich.

172
00:13:12.438 --> 00:13:19.750
<v USSIN>Is there anything else that you want to add that’s just really important before we . . .?

173
00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:31.938
Getting close to time, but anything that you really want to have on record about your family and the community that you haven’t already said?

174
00:13:31.938 --> 00:13:35.838
<v RUBIT>Not that I can think of at present.

175
00:13:35.838 --> 00:13:44.612
I could probably think of some things, but I guess I wasn’t really prepared for the interview today.

176
00:13:44.612 --> 00:13:49.000
<v SHEARMAN>Ms. Prater, do you approve of our project?

177
00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:49.596
<v RUBIT>Yes.

178
00:13:49.596 --> 00:13:50.787
I do.

179
00:13:50.787 --> 00:13:51.383
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

180
00:13:51.383 --> 00:13:52.574
Thank you.

181
00:13:52.574 --> 00:13:53.170
<v RUBIT>Yes.

182
00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:53.766
<v USSIN>Okay.

183
00:13:53.766 --> 00:13:56.745
Can I wrap it up?

184
00:13:56.745 --> 00:13:57.340
Okay.

185
00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:00.319
Well our time is up.

186
00:14:00.319 --> 00:14:08.064
On behalf of the center and the museum, we appreciate your contribution today.

187
00:14:08.064 --> 00:14:17.000
And you say you don’t have anything else really in closing, or you do?

188
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:17.913
<v RUBIT>No, I don’t.

189
00:14:17.913 --> 00:14:18.217
<v USSIN>Okay.

190
00:14:18.217 --> 00:14:19.435
<v RUBIT>Not at this time.

191
00:14:19.435 --> 00:14:21.261
<v USSIN>Okay well this concludes our interview.

192
00:14:21.261 --> 00:14:26.130
We’ll be in touch about the possibility of setting up a second interview with you later.

193
00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:26.435
<v RUBIT>Okay.

194
00:14:26.435 --> 00:14:27.043
Thank you.

195
00:14:27.043 --> 00:14:27.652
<v USSIN>Thank you.

196
00:14:27.652 --> 00:14:29.478
<v SHEARMAN>Ms. Prater, you still live here?

197
00:14:29.478 --> 00:14:29.783
<v RUBIT>Yes.

198
00:14:29.783 --> 00:14:30.087
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

199
00:14:30.087 --> 00:14:31.000
In Mossville?

200
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:31.250
<v RUBIT>Yes.

201
00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:31.500
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

202
00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:35.500
If you like I can show you where the desk is and we’ll scan your pictures.

203
00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:36.000
<v RUBIT>Okay.

204
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:36.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4418.

205
00:14:36.429 --> 00:14:36.857
End Session I.]
